counterexample:
Let X, Y, and Z be mutually independent random variables, each taking one of three values with equal probability (1/3 each):
X ∈ {3, 5, 7}
Y ∈ {2, 4, 9}
Z ∈ {1, 6, 8}

- Checking if X beats Y: The joint sample space for (X, Y) has 9 equiprobable outcomes. The event X > Y occurs for the outcomes (3,2), (5,2), (5,4), (7,2), and (7,4). This gives 5 out of 9 outcomes. Thus, P(X > Y) = 5/9 > 1/2. So X beats Y.
- Checking if Y beats Z: The event Y > Z occurs for the outcomes (2,1), (4,1), (9,1), (9,6), and (9,8). This is 5 out of 9 outcomes. Thus, P(Y > Z) = 5/9 > 1/2. So Y beats Z.
- Checking if X beats Z: The event X > Z occurs for the outcomes (3,1), (5,1), (7,1), and (7,6). This is 4 out of 9 outcomes. Thus, P(X > Z) = 4/9, which is not strictly greater than 1/2. Therefore, X does not beat Z.

This concrete example shows that X beating Y and Y beating Z does not necessarily imply that X beats Z. The relation is not transitive.
